<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Musa ibn Musa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 862</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Musa ibn Musa was the most powerful member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a>, a family of Muwallad lords in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley. Half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inigo-arista">Íñigo Arista</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, he balanced between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and the Christian kingdoms, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> and calling himself the third king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordono-i-of-asturias">Ordoño I of Asturias</a> defeated him at Albelda in 859.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-musa.jpg" alt="Musa ibn Musa" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arenillas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MoroMuza(2010-10-31).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Musa ibn Musa, nicknamed the Great, descended from Cassius, who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> after the Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. His mother, whose name is not known, was by another husband the mother of the Basque chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inigo-arista">Íñigo Arista</a>, according to the chronicler Ibn Hayyan. His early years are obscure, but he is presumed to have supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> at the Second Battle of Ronceveaux, a battle generally credited with giving birth to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He first appears in 840/41, rising with Íñigo against Emir Abd ar-Rahman II after attacks on Íñigo's lands and the expulsion of a kinsman by the governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>. Over the next decade he rebelled and submitted again and again. In 842, believing himself mistreated in the emir's army, he joined his nephew García Íñiguez of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> in defeating a Cordoban force; the emir's campaigns of 843 and 844 defeated the allies, and in November 844 Musa helped defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raiders who had sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. His brother Yunus ibn Musa stayed loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and fought him for about 30 years.</p><p>In 851/2 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inigo-arista">Íñigo Arista</a> and Abd ar-Rahman II both died, and Musa won a two-day battle near Albelda over Basque or Gascon forces. The new emir, Muhammad, named him Wāli of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-march">Upper March</a>. Holding Zaragoza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, he ruled what was in effect a state from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najera">Nájera</a> to Zaragoza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a>. He attacked Álava for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 855 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and Terrassa on his own account in 856, and later let a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> force pass to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, where they captured García Íñiguez, who then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordono-i-of-asturias">Ordoño I of Asturias</a> against him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Christian sources remembered his defeat at Albelda as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-clavijo">Battle of Clavijo</a>, a battle of legend. In 860 Emir Muhammad removed him as wali and governor. In 862, moving against his son-in-law Azraq ibn Mantil ibn Salim, he attacked Guadalajara and was wounded so badly he could not mount a horse, and he died at Tutila on 26 September 862. The family vanished from politics for a decade, then ruled a shrinking territory for another half-century, until the last of his principality was gone by the end of the 920s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Musa ibn Musa al-Qasawi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ramiro I repelled a Viking raid on the coast in 844, while his son Ordoño I defeated <b>Musa ibn Musa</b> of the Banu Qasi near Albelda in 859, according to the chronicles, and began to resettle León and Astorga.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His power rested on an alliance with the Banu Qasi, a Muslim family of Hispano-Gothic descent ruling Tudela and the middle Ebro, whose leader <b>Musa ibn Musa</b> was Íñigo’s half-brother through their mother.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They balanced between Córdoba, the Franks and the Christian kingdoms, intermarrying with the Arista family of Pamplona, whose founder Íñigo Arista was a half-brother of <b>Musa ibn Musa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Arnedo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.23,-2.1&amp;year=852" aria-label="Arnedo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4499 661 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Musa ibn Musa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4949 931h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4949 931h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.23,-2.1&amp;year=852">What was here in 852 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
